  "preamble": "Voicing of the voiceless alveolar affricate [ts] to [dz]. In medieval Ibero-Romance it is part of the Western Romance lenition of intervocalic consonants: the [ts] from palatalized Latin c before front vowels was voiced between vowels, so Old Spanish and Old Galician-Portuguese have intervocalic z [dz] (dezir, vezino, fazer) against voiceless ç [ts] from geminates and post-consonantal positions. In Russian the same change is synchronic: /ts/ has the voiced allophone [dz] before a voiced obstruent. Western Armenian also voiced the Classical Armenian affricate ծ /t͡s/ to [d͡z] (ծով 'sea' [d͡zov]), while Eastern Armenian keeps [t͡s].",
